From 80cb19a0f5578a29619d96e023d519493ea614db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Substring <frog2wah@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 15:42:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/40] Support hardware rendered OpenGL libretro cores

* Hardware rendered cores (RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_HW_RENDER: OpenGL compatibility/core, OpenGL ES 2/3) get an OpenGL context owned by the libretro driver (libretro_gl), never shared with the MAME renderer, and their frame is read back into a bitmap, so they work with every video backend including kmsraw and MiSTer
* EGL context without surface on a DRM render node (works under X11, Wayland and the KMS console), EGL and GL loaded at run time, the context of an OpenGL MAME renderer (EGL or GLX) is restored after each call to the core
* The frame is read back in the frame it's rendered, adding no frame of latency: persistent mapped buffer (GL_ARB_buffer_storage) when available, else pixel buffer object, else direct glReadPixels; the GPU is waited for with glFinish, fence waits stalled for ~500 ms at times on r600
* New options: -libretro_gl_device selects the rendering GPU by render node, -libretro_gl_readback forces a readback method, timings logged with -verbose
* Vulkan and Direct3D cores, and hardware rendering on Windows and macOS, aren't supported yet
* Tested with Flycast (Virtua Tennis 2) on a Radeon RX 6600 and a Radeon HD 5750, in a window with the OpenGL renderer

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
